			<div class="content" id="post-content-4854344">
									

<p>Heyo followup! :)</p>
<p><strong>the sand was very&#8230;&#8230; SANDY</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: Janual Grey is a mixed-race of a human and a Lovecraftian-horror named &quot;Darkness&quot;. He's identified to be a reality-altering eldritch abomination that lives his life as a regular teenager.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main issue here is the vagueness of the effects, &quot;realty altering&quot; is too broad to really describe what the skip does, as it doesn't give any specifics or boundaries. The anomaly the skip has, also doesn't seem to relate much to its purpose (or intended purpose), which makes it hard to really get invested in the concept, as the main elements feel disjointed.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>After receiving witness reports from a family, claiming that while they were in the middle of driving on the mountain highways to visit a family member, the car had some issues, and they've accidentally crashed through the guardrails and are now half-way to their death. They were trying to find a way to safely get out of the car when one of them noticed that something behind the car is approaching them, when it arrives it moved the car back on the road before spontaneously disappeared. As the person who witnessed its appearances, the youngest daughter, said that she saw a monster that saved them. When this was brought to the Foundation. They knew they have an anomaly on the chase, the location of its presence is identified to be somewhere near the woods of Acadia National Park, and soon an MTF was dispatched to detained the SCP. It wasn't shortly after they've discovered and been able to detain it since it means no harm, and it was transported back to Site-17.</p>
<p>Based on the Foundation's logs, the interview log, the SCP states its real name is &quot;Janual Grey,&quot; and he's a reality-altering anomaly identified as the male gender. When he first discovered his powers, he was in the middle of camping at the same location where he was found, which happens after he blackouts and finds himself in an unknown location of the woods but has no idea of what could the caused the blackout to be. Afterward, the Foundation puts upon him a few testings to determined his boundaries. The following logs describe what methods they used to demonstrate his anomalous properties, what examination tests they've put out for him, and what SCP they've used on him. And an incident logs where a group of a religious cult named &quot;Allies of &quot;Darkness&quot; broke into his containment unit and trying to convince him to follow them to meet his father, named &quot;Darkness&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is far too detailed for the pitch, major plot elements shouldn't be sacrificed in favour of specific details. Currently the narrative feels unfinished as it missing a payoff/resolution and main conflict, currently the story doesn't build up to anything, or really show anything more than &quot;Foundation finds something, then they test it&quot;. Due to the lack of clear arc, the story feels un-engaging.</p>
<p>Overall, I would recommend simplifying this and only showing the main arc and conflict, along with this, I'd also recommend to take the story in a more unique direction, rather than juts relying on the &quot;Foundation finds and tests something&quot; storyline (which at this point has become a cliche). You may have an easier time developing the narrative if you get a idea of where you want it to end.</p>
<p>Generally, the narrative pitch should include the most important elements of the story. You're looking to give an idea of how the draft will look and/or what direction you want to take the story.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to afore mentioned concerns, no greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>-RED</p>

